# basic use of vim

i to insert (before the char)
a to append (after the char)
x to delete the char

cmd
    -:wq to save the file and exit
    -:q! to trash the changes and exit directly
    -dw to delete a word (from the cursor to the end of the world)

<backspace> to delete a char before the block cursor

cmd
    -d$ delete to the end of the line
    the commands made of two parts
    that is operator and motion
    d (operator) w (motion)
    d
    -w to the start of the next word (brace is infered to a word)
    -e to the end of this word
    -$ to the end of this line

    w to the start of next word (space char is not regarded as a word)
        maintain the start char
    e to the end of this word
        maintain the char after the end
    number + operator do many times
    2w move twice
    0 move to the start of the line

    d number motion (delete several words)
    dw cursor on the space char will regard this space char as a word
        like |world    |world
    de do not obey this rule, delete the operator(.,;) and the space char
        which will be seen as a part of next word
        like |    world|   world

    dd to delete a line
    2dd to delete the following two lines

    u to undo the last command
    U to fix the whole line

    ctrl+R undo the undos (act as ctrl+Y)

    p to paste the last deleted text after cursor  (use dd dw d$)
        (the deleted text will be restored for the p command)

    r + char to replace the char on the cursor

    ce to change until the end of a word (act like de and i,
        but you cant insert any char before the char you press ce)
    cc does the same to whole line (act as dd and i, but dont delete \n)

    c number motion
    cw c$(end of line) the motions are the same
    cw (including the space)
    ce (dont include)
    c$ to the end of the line



    ctrl G to shoe the position in the file
    G to move to the bottom of the file
    gg to move to the start of the file
    number G skip to the line
    number gg   the same

    / + phrase + <enter> to search
    % place the cursor on a ( [ { and this will find the matching

    :s substitude command on the line
    :s/old/new
    :s/old/new/g(global)
    :#line#,#line#s/... set line range

    :%s/... search whole file
    :$s/old/new/g(for global) gc(for a prompt to ensure every change)

    / search forward
    ? search backward
    both will search from the opposite side when hit

